This is the first time I post something to usenet, so I apologize in advance if
this message reaches you in a scrambled condition :-) I've been playing nethack
for several months, but had never occured to me that there might be a newsgroup
for nethack until I ate this fortune cookie ;-)

Anyway, the first time I played nethack I was very disappointed to see that male
and female characters are treated exactly the same (ditto for cat vs dog). As
compared to a more "realistic" approach (I suppose some people might say
"sexist" instead ;-) ), this reduces the number of options available to the
player while costing both programming time and extra memory. Let's try to be
sensible, what's the point of having a 'female' flag if the only thing it does
is give you incubi instead of succubi (interestingly enough, nymphs can charm
both sexes!). Surely nobody on this conference will suggest that female players
(I mean the human beings running the program) feel more offended at the notion
that they are running a male character which is being charmed by a female being
(which is what would happen if the 'female' option was not available) than at
the realization that their female character is being seduced by a female nymph!
Similarly, I was surprised to see a pet cat fetching objects so diligently
that was before I met my first shop and understood why it is so important for
the sake of the game to have cats fetch things as well.

Ok, I can hear the clamour, "very well, but do you have a proposal?" Well, I
don't have a firm proposal, because there are A LOT of possibilities, but I have
some ideas which we could discuss.

The game could be changed to have *both* incubi and succubi in any given
dungeon. If you meet the one of same sex, it just fights you in the same way it
fights your pet. Shopkeepers could make better prices for players of opposite
sex with a good charisma, as in real life (I'm not saying it's a commendable
thing to do, I'm saying it's a fact of life). Now, from this point what would
we give to female players in order to compensate for the lower strength (apart
from the relatively marginal ability of laying eggs)? One possibility is to make
them immune to nymphs, which is after all quite logical. Unfortunately this
would probably mean that male is better at the lower dungeon levels and female
has more advantages for powerful characters. This immunity could be traded
against an intrinsic aggravate_nymphs (jealousy); the nymph would retain her
power to steal objects and teleport away when she hits you, but lose her ability
to seduce you into giving away your armour, etc. So you would never hand out
your armour and end up naked in the middle of a morgue, but on the other hand
you'd never get a chance to quietly walk away and she might still steal other
things from your pack. At the risk of being called sexist, I would like to
mention another possibility: simply increase the percentage of male shopkeepers
in the dungeon; males get better strength, females get better value-for-money
in the shops, that sounds fair enough to me.

Concerning the pets, I have always found it frustrating not to be able to
whistle for my dog when it's wandering somewhere in the dungeon. I know it's
probably a good amount of code, but I would like to propose a significant
addition to the pet routines: the ability for the player to give orders to its
pets, as it would in real life (note that this does NOT mean the pet will do as
ordered! - again, as in real life :-) ). Basically what I want to tell the dog
is either "follow me", "stay where you are", "come back to me", "fight/don't
fight" or "fetch". Your average 20th-century dog knows about all that - and is
usually very good at ignoring "stay where you are, you can't enter the shop ya
know" as well as "don't fight the lady's dog, I know it's very small but it
ain't a cat, I promise!" (why would we waste money buying leashes otherwise?).
It isn't as hard to implement as it may sound: for each class of monster you
have to define two more variables, intelligence and obedience (an order will
be carried out if it is understood, if the pet is tame enough and if it feels
like doing it - the latter being a function of your luck and the pet's
obedience and taming level). If you give the pet a "frustrating" command like
"stay here" when it obviously doesn't want to (as opposed to "follow me" or
"fetch" which it might enjoy doing), it will decrease its taming level, so the
facility won't be abused; in any case, the probability that a dog will "stay
here" while you fetch a tripe ration you want to keep to get another pet is
very low :-) The routine that decides what the pet is going to do "normally"
can be slightly altered to decide how "strongly" the pet wants to do it, and
this can be easily compared to what you are asking it to do in order to find
out whether it's something it will be happy to do or not.

Now, what would this mean in practice, as compared to what the game is now?
Well, dogs are more obedient and you could expect them to come back when you
whistle with a much higher probability than cats (this would, of course, wake
up all the monsters within range). On the other hand dogs tend to bark
spontaneously and often uncontrollably, whereas cats are very stealthy: your
dog might wake up monsters in the rooms it visits while a cat would not. A cat
will also see much better in the dark, which can be an advantage at the lower
levels (ie the code which decides what it's going to do could take into account
things you don't see, or the cat could even yowl to warn you of danger you are
not seeing). Well, this is all a matter of balance, you would have to try the
resulting game out and see how it behaves. If cats are still too weak at the
first levels, you could give them a big bonus against rats and lizards, which
are very common in the first dungeon levels (dogs could get a bonus against
felines, which are much less common). As for polymorphed pets, they would be
just as they are now, with gray oozes not understanding what you're trying to
tell them, demons understanding but doing what they please, and humanoids
reluctantly carrying out orders until they get fed up and decide to hit you on
the head instead (now, hang on, I had a pet minotaur with 651 (count'em) hit
points in my last game, I hate to think what might have happened to me if he
had decided I was pissing him off, especially seeing how he had so efficiently
dispatched a brand new, freshly unpacked white (adult) dragon popping out of
nowhere rather than minding his own business - did that last 2 or 3 rounds? I
can't remember... ;-) )

Anyway, these are only ideas. I really enjoy nethack (especially since I got
PL9 and no longer get killed every time I eat something below dungeon level 20
- that was PL2, and a VERY frustrating problem indeed!), I am just a bit
unhappy over the apparent lack of justification for male vs female and cat vs
dog (personally, I wish I could always get a cat: there aren't as many f's on
the first 5 levels as there are d's, and using / to avoid losing 1 turn *is*
a pain!). In any case, keep up the good work! :-)

  Eric


